  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Peace Studies Pakistan-Afghanistan</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project seeks to contribute to the peaceful resolution of the ongoing Afghan conflict. The US-Taliban deal in Feb 2020 created some hope for political settlement and peace in Afghanistan, but over a year later the initial excitement has given way to uncertainty, renewing fears if the country is returning to the 1990s when it was ravaged by horrific civil wars. The situation in Afghanistan has a direct bearing on Pakistan&#x2019;s internal security and stability, thus Pakistan needs a fresh perspective on its western neighbour. Such a perspective must be holistic, and informed not only by the military, but also the wider state and society. To help inform the Pakistani government&#x2019;s policies and actions about the Afghan peace process, this project seeks to develop an inclusive response through a well-thought out mechanism involving extensive mapping and analysis of diverse viewpoints of Pakistan&#x2019;s political leaders, military, print and electronic as well as social media, civil society, religious establishment, secular segments, and the ethnic groups inhabiting both Pakistan and Afghanistan and having stakes in the Afghan peace process such as the Pashtun, Hazara, Baloch etc. The findings will feed into and inform quarterly dialogue sessions with experts, opinion makers, policy analysts, and civil and military policy makers and practitioners who will deliberate upon Pakistan&#x2019;s position, short- and long-term interests, and policy options. Ultimately, the project will produce a comprehensive guide for the Pakistani government on how to secure Pakistan&#x2019;s vital interests in Afghanistan while contributing to sustainable peace at the same time.</narrative>
  </description>
